  heroHeadline: "AI Receptionists That Answer Every Call, Book the Job and Know When to Put a Person On",
  heroTagline: "We build AI voice agents for Australian businesses: phone agents that answer on your Australian number, take bookings in your calendar or job system, capture the details your team needs, and transfer to a person when a caller needs one. Recording, consent and privacy are designed in from the first call.",
  summaryStatement: "An AI voice agent, often called an AI receptionist, is software that answers or makes phone calls in natural spoken language: it listens to the caller, works out what they need, looks up or updates your systems, books appointments or jobs, and hands the call or a written summary to a person when the request is outside what it should handle.",
  description: [
    "Most small and medium businesses lose calls in the same places: after hours, at lunch, when the only person who can answer is on a roof, in a consult or driving. A voicemail box catches some of them, and the rest ring a competitor. An AI voice agent answers every call on the first ring, at any hour, and handles the routine parts of the conversation the way a well trained receptionist would: it greets the caller in your business name, asks the right questions, checks availability, books the appointment or job, and sends the caller and your team a confirmation. When a call needs a human, it says so and transfers the caller, or takes a message with enough detail that your team can call back prepared.",
    "The technology is the same agent design we use for AI chatbots and AI agents, with a telephony layer in front. A phone number from an Australian carrier or a voice platform routes the call to speech recognition, which turns the caller's words into text; a language model decides what to say and which tools to use; and a speech synthesis voice replies. The hard part is not getting a demo to talk. It is latency, interruptions, Australian accents and background noise on a mobile in a ute, and making the agent reliable when it books into a real calendar, ServiceM8, simPRO, a CRM such as HubSpot or GoHighLevel, or practice management software.",
    "Phone calls carry legal obligations that a chat widget does not. Under the federal Telecommunications (Interception and Access) Act 1979, interception means listening to or recording a communication passing over a telecommunications system without the knowledge of the person making it, so the agent tells every caller at the start that the call is recorded and why. State and territory surveillance and listening device laws differ: the Australian Law Reform Commission noted that Queensland, Victoria and the Northern Territory allow a party to a conversation to record it, while the other jurisdictions do not have that general exception. Because callers can ring from any state, we design for the strictest rule: announce recording up front, give the caller a way to continue without it, and record the disclosure in the call log. Your lawyer confirms how the rules apply to your business; this page is general information, not legal advice.",
    "Privacy comes next. Recordings and transcripts are personal information, and for health businesses they are often health information. We design to the Australian Privacy Principles, collect only what the call needs, set retention periods, and can host transcripts and recordings in Australian cloud regions. If your data must stay in Australia, tell us before you sign and we write it into your Schedule of Work. Senior engineers lead every build and review every change, and you receive updates every Tuesday and Friday, Sydney time, so you can hear the agent improving week by week.",
  ],
